In this proof, the constants $C$ may depend on $r$ and $n$. Since any Gaussian $(1,\infty)$-atom is also a Gaussian $(1,r)$-atom, $H^{1,\infty}(\gamma)$ is a subspace of $H^{1,r}(\gamma)$ and $
orm{f}{H^{1,r}(\gamma)}\le
orm{f}{H^{1,\infty}(\gamma)}$. Conversely, suppose that $a$ is a Gaussian $(1,r)$-atom associated to the ball $B \in \mathcal{B}_1$. Then the function $a\,\gamma_0$ is a multiple of a Lebesgue $(1,r)$-atom. Indeed, $\int a\gamma_0\wrt\lambda=\int a\wrt\gamma=0$ and, by the equivalence of the Gauss and Lebesgue measures on admissible balls, $$ 
orm{a\gamma_0}{L^r(\lambda)}\le C\,\lambda(B)^{1/r-1}. $$ Hence, $a_0\gamma$ is in $H^1(\lambda)$ with norm at most $C$. By Lemma ef{cptsupp}, it has a decomposition $$ a\,\gamma_0=\sum_j\lambda_j \alpha_j, $$ where each $\alpha_j$ is a Lebesgue $(1,\infty)$-atom associated to a ball $B_j$ contained in $2B$. Moreover $$ \sum_j\mod{\lambda_j}\le C, $$ and each $B_j$ is admissible at scale $2$. Define $a_j=\alpha_j\,\gamma_0^{-1}$. Then $\int a_j\wrt\gamma=0$, and by the equivalence of the Gauss and Lebesgue measures on $B_j$  egin{align*} 
orm{a_j}{\infty}\le C \gamma(B_j)^{-1}. \end{align*} Thus the $a_j$ are multiples of Gaussian $(1,\infty)$-atoms. Since $a=\sum_j\lambda_j a_j$, we conclude that $a \in H^{1,\infty}(\gamma)$ and  egin{equation} 
orm{a}{H^{1,\infty}(\gamma)}\le\,C \sum_j\mod{\lambda_j}\le C. \end{equation}

Let $f$ be a function in $L^1(\gamma)$ such that $\mlo f\in L^1(\gamma)$ and $E_+(f)<~\infty$. Write $f=c(f)+f_0$, where $c(f)=\int f\wrt\gamma$. Since $c(f)$ is a multiple of the exceptional atom, it suffices to find an atomic decomposition of $f_0$. Note that $f_0$ satisfies $$ \mlo f_0 \in L^1(\gamma) \qquad	extrm{and}\qquad \int\mod{x}^2\, \mod{f_0(x)}\wrt\gamma(x)<\infty. $$\par Let $\set{B_j}$ be the covering of $\BR^n$ by maximal admissible balls and $\set{\eta_j}$ the corresponding partition of unity introduced in the proof of Theorem ef{char}. As there, we choose numbers $b_j\in\BC$ such that $$ \int_{-\infty}^\infty (f_0-b_j)\eta_j \wrt \gamma =0 \qquad orall j. $$ Then the argument leading to (ef{aat}) shows that $$ f_0=\sum_j \sum_k \lambda_{jk} \,a_{jk} +\sum_j b_j \eta_j, $$ where the $a_{jk}$ are Gaussian atoms supported in $4B_j$ and $$ \sum_{j,k} \mod{\lambda_{jk}}\le\,C\, 
orm{\mlo f_0}{L^1(\gamma)}. $$ It remains only to prove that $\sum_j b_j \eta_j$ is in $\huat {\gamma}$. We write $g_j=b_j \eta_j$ and observe that  egin{equation} \int \sum_j g_j\wrt\gamma=0 \end{equation} because $f_0$ and the $a_{ij}$ have integrals zero. Thus $$ \sum_j g_j=\sum_j\Big(g_j-\int g_j\wrt\gamma \,\phi_0\Big), $$ where $\phi_0$ is as in Lemma ef{trch}. Since (ef{1st}) remains valid for $f_0$, we have  egin{equation} 
orm{g_j}{\infty}\le C\, rac{1}{\gamma(B_j)} \int_{B_j}\mod{f_0}\wrt \gamma. \end{equation} Lemma ef{trch} thus applies to each $g_j$, and using also the bounded overlap of the $B_j$ we conclude  egin{align*} 
orm{\sum_j g_j}{H^1(\gamma)}\,\le \, C\ \sum_j ig(1+\mod{c_{B_j}}^2 ig)\ \int_{B_j} \mod{f_0}\wrt\gamma \,\le \, C\,\int  ig(1+\mod{x}^2 ig)\, \mod{f_0}\wrt\gamma. \end{align*} This concludes the proof of the sufficiency and the norm estimate. \par The necessity of the condition $\mlo f\in L^1(\gamma)$ was obtained in Lemma ef{necn}. To prove the necessity of (ef{nec}), let $0 \le f \in \huat{\gamma}$. We first observe that the function $x\mapsto \mod{x}^2$ is in $BMO(\ga)$. Indeed, its oscillation on any admissible ball is bounded. Since $BMO(\gamma)$ is a lattice, the functions $g_k(x)=\min\set{\mod{x}^2,k}$ are in $BMO(\gamma)$, uniformly for $k \ge 1$. By the monotone convergence theorem and the duality between $\huat{\gamma}$ and $BMO(\gamma)$, $$ \int \mod{x}^2 f(x)\wrt\gamma(x)=\lim_k \int g_k(x) f(x)\wrt\gamma(x) \le C 
orm{f}{\huat{\gamma}}. $$ The theorem is proved.

Let~$K$ be a number field such that $\Acal\subset K$. For~$\a\in\Acal$ and~$v\in M_K$, we let \[ \a_v =  egin{cases} \a&	ext{if $\|\a\|_v\le1$,}\ \a^{-1}&	ext{if $\|\a\|_v>1$,}\ \end{cases} \] so in particular $\|\a_v\|_v\le 1$. We now compute  egin{align} (n\log m)\D(\Acal,m,n) &= \sum_{\a\in\Acal}\sum_{v\mid m} v(\a^n-1) &&	ext{def. of $\D(\Acal,m,n)$,} 
otag\ &\le \sum_{\a\in\Acal}\sum_{v\in M_K^0} v(\a^n-1) &&	ext{since $\a\in ar\ZZ$,} 
otag\ &= \sum_{\a\in\Acal}\sum_{v\in M_K^\infty} \log\|\a^n-1\|_v &&	ext{product rule,} 
otag\ &\le \sum_{\a\in\Acal}\sum_{\substack{v\in M_K^\infty\\|\a\|_v>1\}} \log\|\a^n\|_v + \sum_{\a\in\Acal}\sum_{v\in M_K^\infty} \log\|\a_v^n-1\|_v \hidewidth 
otag\ &= n\sum_{\a\in\Acal} h(\a) + \sum_{\a\in\Acal}\sum_{v\in M_K^\infty} \log\|\a_v^n-1\|_v. \hidewidth \end{align} We now replace~$\Acal$ with~$\Acal^j$. Then using $h(\a^j)=jh(\a)$ and Proposition~ef{prop:DAjmngeDAmn}, which says that $\D(\Acal,m,n)\le\D(\Acal^j,m,n)$, we find that \[ (n\log m)\D(\Acal,m,n) \le nj\sum_{\a\in\Acal}h(\a) + \sum_{\a\in\Acal}\sum_{v\in M_K^\infty} \log\|\a_v^{jn}-1\|_v . \] We multiply by the Fej'er multiplier $1- rac{j}{J+1}$ and sum over $1\le j\le J$ to obtain  egin{multline*}  rac{Jn\log m}{2}\D(\Acal,m,n) \le  rac{(J^2+2J)n}{6}\sum_{\a\in\Acal}h(\a) \* + \sum_{\a\in\Acal}\sum_{v\in M_K^\infty} \sum_{j=1}^J \left(1- rac{j}{J+1}ight)\log\|\a_v^{jn}-1\|_v . \end{multline*} Note that the sum over~$v$ is over archimedean absolute values, so if we assume that~$\a_v$ is chosen in the unit disk to maximize the innermost sum over~$j$, we get the estimate  egin{multline*}  rac{Jn\log m}{2}\D(\Acal,m,n) \* \le  rac{(J^2+2J)n}{6}\sum_{\a\in\Acal}h(\a) + |\Acal| \sup_{\substack{z\in\CC\|z|\le1\}} \sum_{j=1}^J \left(1- rac{j}{J+1}ight)\log|z^j-1|. \end{multline*} We can now use Proposition~ef{prop:fejarestimate} to conclude that \[  rac{Jn\log m}{2}\D(\Acal,m,n) \le  rac{(J^2+2J)n}{6}\sum_{\a\in\Acal}h(\a) +  rac{|\Acal|}{2}\left(\log\left( rac{J}{2}+1ight)+1ight). \] After a little bit of algebra, we obtain the desired result.

