to g_{f, \zeta_0}(\sqrt{h})$ is strictly increasing on $(0,1]$. For all $	heta_1,	heta_2\in\Theta$, define $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}\dvtx(0,1]	o(0,\infty)$ by $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(w):=h_{1,w,	heta_2,1}(1)/h_{1,w,	heta_1,1}(1)$ for $0 < w \le 1$. In particular, taking $d=1$ and $\zeta=\zeta_0$ in \eqref{centralidentitynoneq}, we must have\vspace*{-1pt}  egin{equation} g_{f,\zeta_0}  igl\{\sqrt{H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(1)}  igr\} = \int_{(0,1)} g_{f,\zeta_0}  igl\{\sqrt{H_{	heta_1,	heta _2}(w)}  igr\}\,\mathrm{d}w\vspace*{-2pt} \end{equation} for all $	heta_1,	heta_2\in\Theta$. (i) and (ii) For $i=1,2,$ let $	heta_i=(h_{0,i}, eta_i,\alpha,\lambda)\in\Theta$. Then,\vspace*{-1pt} \[ h^2_{1,w,	heta_1,1}(1) \mathrm{e}^{\alpha w}  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}w}H_{	heta _1,	heta_2}(w) =  eta_2 h_{0,1}- eta_1 h_{0,2},\qquad 0< w\le 1 .\vspace*{-2pt} \] (Note that this formula extends to $\alpha=0$.) If $ eta_1 =  eta_2 > 0$ and $h_{0,1} > h_{0,2}$, then $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}$ is strictly increasing with $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(1) \le1$, contradicting \eqref{centralidentitynoneq2} since $h\mapsto g_{f, \zeta_0}(\sqrt{h})$ is strictly increasing on $(0,1]$. If $h_{0,1} = h_{0,2}$ and $ eta_2 <  eta_1$, then $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}$ is strictly decreasing with $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(0+)=1$, contradicting \eqref{centralidentitynoneq2} since $h\mapsto g_{f, \zeta_0}(\sqrt{h})$ is strictly increasing on $(0,1]$. Reversing the roles of parameters by replacing $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}$ with $H_{	heta_2,	heta_1}$, the previous reasoning extends to the remaining cases where either $ eta_1 =  eta_2 > 0$ and $h_{0,1} < h_{0,2}$, or $h_{0,1} = h_{0,2}$ and $ eta_2 >  eta_1$. This completes the proof of (i) and (ii). (iii) If $(h_{0}, eta,\alpha_1,\lambda),(h_{0}, eta,\alpha_2,\lambda)\in \Theta$ and $ eta=0$, then we have $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(w)=\mathrm{e}^{(\alpha_1-\alpha_2)w}$ for all $w\in(0,1]$. (Note that this formula extends to $\alpha_1=0$ or $\alpha_2=0$.) By the same arguments as in parts (i) and (ii), we get from \eqref{centralidentitynoneq2} that $\alpha_1=\alpha_2$. (iv) In view of (iii), we may assume that $ eta>0$. Contradicting the hypothesis, we assume that $\alpha_2>0$. It follows from the strict inequality $\mathrm{e}^x-1>x$, $x>0$, that\vspace*{-1pt}  egin{eqnarray*} (h_0+ eta w)^2  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}w}H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(w) &=&\mathrm{e}^{-\alpha_2w}  iggl\{w( eta^2 - \alpha_2 eta h_0)-h_0^2\alpha_2 -  rac{ eta^2}{\alpha_2} (\mathrm{e}^{\alpha_2w} - 1)  iggr\}\[-1pt] &<&-\alpha_2 h_0 \mathrm{e}^{-\alpha_2 w}(h_0+w eta) < 0\vspace*{-2pt} \end{eqnarray*} for all $w\in(0,1]$. Thus, $w\mapsto H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(w)$ is strictly decreasing on $(0,1]$ with $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(0+)=1$, contradicting \eqref{centralidentitynoneq2}. Thus, we must have $\alpha_2=0$.\vadjust{\goodbreak} (v) Let $(h_{0}, eta,\alpha_1,\lambda),(h_{0}, eta,\alpha_2,\lambda)\in \Theta$ with $\alpha_{2}>\alpha_{1}$. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $ eta > 0$. First, assume that $ eta/\alpha_2\le h_0\le  eta/\alpha_1$. Then, $ eta-\alpha_1 h_0\ge0$ and $ eta-\alpha_2 h_0\le0$. Note that we cannot simultaneously have that $ eta - \alpha_1 h_0= eta - \alpha_2 h_0= 0$ such that \[ h_{1,w,	heta_1,1}^2  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}w}H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(w) = ( eta - \alpha_2h_0)\mathrm{e}^{-\alpha_2 w}h_{1,w,	heta_1,1} -( eta - \alpha_1h_0)\mathrm{e}^{-\alpha_1 w}h_{1,w,	heta_2,1}<0 \] for all $0<w\le1$. Consequently, $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}$ is strictly decreasing with $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(0+)=1$, contradicting \eqref{centralidentitynoneq2}. Second, let $h_0< eta/\alpha_2$ and set \[ \psi(w):= ( eta-\alpha_2h_0)h_{1,w,	heta_1,1} -( eta-\alpha_1h_0)\mathrm{e}^{-(\alpha_1-\alpha_2) w}h_{1,w,	heta_2,1},\qquad 0<w\le1 . \] As we have $\alpha_2>\alpha_1$ and $h_0< eta/\alpha_2$, we must have that $ eta - \alpha_1h_0> eta - \alpha_2h_0>0$ such~that \[ \psi'(w)=(\alpha_1 - \alpha_2)( eta - \alpha_1h_0)\mathrm{e}^{-(\alpha _1 - \alpha_2) w}h_{d,w,	heta_2,1}<0,\qquad 0<w\le1 . \] Note that $\psi(0+)=(\alpha_1 - \alpha_2) h_0^2<0$ and thus $\psi(w)<0$ for all $0<w\le1$. Since reak $\mathrm{e}^{\alpha_2w }h_{d,w,	heta_1,1}^2  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}w}H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(w)=\psi(w)<0$ for all $0 < w \le 1$, $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}$ is strictly decreasing with $H_{	heta_1,	heta_2}(0+)=1$, contradicting \eqref{centralidentitynoneq2}. This completes the proof of (v).\

Let us denote a given instance of monotone $\oneinthree$ by a pair of a set $U$ of Boolean variables and a set of clauses of three literals, which are positive due to the monotonicity. We will show a polynomial-time reduction from this to an instance of quadripartite $\oneinthree$. The reduction first transforms each clause of the given $\oneinthree$ instance into a quadripartite conjunction of clauses while preserving the $\oneinthree$ satisfiability, and then conjuncts them. Each conjunction is designed so that it admits a partition of its variables into four disjoint sets one of which contains all variables of its source clause. The $i$-th clause $\{x, y, z\}$ is converted into the following conjunction of 13 clauses:  egin{equation}  egin{array}{l} \{
eg x, a_1, b_1\} \{
eg y, a_2, b_2\} \{
eg z, a_3, b_3\} \{a_1, a_2, a_3\} \ \hspace*{15mm} \{
eg x, c, d_x\} \{
eg y, c, d_y\} \{
eg z, c, d_z\} \{c, e_{xy}. f_{xy}\} \{c, e_{yz}. f_{yz}\} \ \hspace*{30mm} \{c, e_{zx}. f_{zx}\} \{d_x, d_y, e_{xy}\} \{d_y, d_z, e_{yz}\} \{d_z, d_x, e_{zx}\}, \end{array} \end{equation} where all variables but $
eg x, 
eg y, 
eg z$ are introduced exclusively for this clause. This transformation introduces the negated literals $
eg x, 
eg y, 
eg z$ but they do not cause any problem because after all clauses being converted in this manner, no positive literals in the given $\oneinthree$ instance remains. We claim that this preserves the $\oneinthree$ satisfiability. If the $i$-th clause is $\oneinthree$ satisfied, then due to the symmetry among $x, y, z$ in \eqref{eq:quad_1in3SAT_conjunction}, it suffices to examine the case $x = 1, y = z = 0$. Then this conjunction is $\oneinthree$ satisfied by setting $a_1, d_x, e_{yz}, f_{xy}, f_{zx}$ to be 1 and the others to be 0. If $x = y = z = 0$, then the first three clauses in \eqref{eq:quad_1in3SAT_conjunction} imply $a_1 = a_2 = a_3 = 0$, but then the fourth clause cannot be satisfied. In the case $x = y = 1$, we consider two cases depending on the value of $c$. If $c = 0$, then $d_x = d_y = 1$ must hold in order to satisfy $\{
eg x, c, d_x\}$ and $\{
eg y, c, d_y\}$, but then the clause $\{d_x, d_y, e_{xy}\}$ contains too many true variables. Otherwise, $d_x = d_y = e_{xy} = 0$, but then the clause cannot be satisfied. Therefore, the clause $\{x, y, z\}$ is $\oneinthree$ satisfiable if and only if so are all clauses in \eqref{eq:quad_1in3SAT_conjunction}. The conjunction \eqref{eq:quad_1in3SAT_conjunction} is actually not quadripartite yet, and hence, needs further transformation. We replace its last clause $\{d_z, d_x, e_{zx}\}$ using the conversion  egin{equation} \{\alpha,  eta, \gamma\} = \{
eg \alpha, h, k\}\{
eg  eta, i, k\}\{
eg \gamma, j, k\}\{h, i, j\}, \end{equation} where $h, i, j, k$ are auxiliary variables introduced exclusively for this conversion. This conversion preserves the $\oneinthree$ satisfiability and quadripartite property ootnote{ We have not applied this (simple) conversion directly to the $i$-th clause. This is because the variables involved in the conjunction thus obtained cannot be divided into four sets such that one of them contains all of $
eg x, 
eg y, 
eg z$. }. A problem is that the resulting conjunction contains both positive and negative literals of $d_z, d_x, e_{zx}$. Thus, to each of the four clauses that replaced $\{d_z, d_x, e_{zx}\}$, the same conversion must apply further. In this way, we finally obtain a conjunction of 28 clauses that admits quartering its variables as follows:  egin{eqnarray*} U_{i,1} &=& \{
eg x, 
eg y, 
eg z, e_{xy}, e_{yz}, e_{zx}\}, \ U_{i,2} &=& \{a_1, b_3, c\}, \ U_{i,3} &=& \{a_2, b_1, d_x, d_z, f_{xy}, f_{yz}, f_{zx}\}, \ U_{i,4} &=& \{a_3, b_2, d_y\}, \end{eqnarray*} where the variables introduced via the conversion of last clause are omitted. Observe that the (negated) literals of all variables in the given SAT instance is in the same set $U_{i, 1}$, and that $(U_{i, 2} \cup U_{i, 3} \cup U_{i, 4}) \cap (U_{j, 2} \cup U_{j, 3} \cup U_{j, 4}) = \emptyset$ for any $1 \le i < j \le n$. These two properties immediately bring us the quartering of variables occurring in the resulting conjunction as $U_k =  igcup_{1 \le i \le n} U_{i, k}$ for each $k \in \{1, 2, 3, 4\}$.

Using this instance, now we can prove that $ indoptstrength$ is \NP-hard. Making use of the fact that all of its $	au$-inequalities contain at most 4 terms, we transform them into cooperation sets of a strength-free TAS. The inequalities \eqref{eq:TP_jth_clause_lessthan_tau}, which are for the clause $c_j$, are encoded as $t_{c_j} = (v_{j_1}, v_{j_2}, v_{j_3}, x_	au)$ with $\mathcal{D}(t_{c_j}) = \mathcal{P}(\{
orth, \west, \south, \east\}) \setminus \{\{
orth\}, \{\west\}, \{\south\}, \{
orth, \west\}, \{
orth, \south\}, \{\west, \south\}\}$. Note that its east side is labeled with an auxiliary variable $x_	au$, but this variable does not play any essential role but filling the blank side (the tile is not triangle but square). Likewise, the inequalities that aim at forcing $v_1, \ldots, v_n$ be positive are converted. The inequalities in \eqref{eq:positive} are encoded as a tile type $t_i = (x_1, x_a, x_	au, x_	au)$ with $\mathcal{D}(t_i) = \mathcal{P}(\{
orth, \west, \south, \east\}) \setminus \{\{
orth\}, \{\west\}\}$. Though being not mentioned in \eqref{eq:positive}, we can assume $x_1 < 	au$ as noted in Remark~ef{rmk:strictly_lessthan_tau}. The inequality \eqref{eq:TP_x1x2} is encoded as $t' = (x_1, x_2, x_	au, x_	au)$ with $\mathcal{D}(t') = \mathcal{P}(\{
orth, \west, \south, \east\}) \setminus \{\{
orth\}, \{\west\}, \{
orth, \west\}\}$. Then, there exists a TAS at temperature 4 that is locally equivalent to this strength-free TAS if and only if the given instance of $\oneinthree$ is satisfiable.

By Theorem ef{thm+chen}, it suffices to show that $(\log G(x))''$ is a completely monotonic function for $x\geq0$. Let $ g(x)=\log G(x).$ So  egin{eqnarray} &&[g(x)]^{(n)}
onumber\ &=&[\log\Gamma(n_{0}+ax+1)]^{(n)}-[\log{\Gamma(k_{0}+bx+1)}]^{(n)}-[\log{\Gamma(\overline{k_{0}}+x\overline{b}+1)}]^{(n)}
onumber\ &=&(-1)^n\int_{0}^{\infty} rac{t^{n-1}}{1-e^{-t}}\left[a^ne^{-t(n_{0}+ax+1)}-b^ne^{-t(k_{0}+bx+1)}-\overline{b}^ne^{-t(\overline{k_{0}}+x\overline{b}+1)}ight]dt\ &=&(-1)^n\int_{0}^{\infty}a^nt^{n-1}e^{-tax}\left[ rac{e^{-(n_0+1)t}}{1-e^{-t}}- rac{e^{-ta(k_{0}+1)/b}}{1-e^{-at/b}}- rac{e^{-ta(\overline{k_{0}}+1)/\overline{b}}}{1-e^{-at/\overline{b}}}ight]dt
onumber \end{eqnarray} since $$[\log\Gamma(x)]^{(n)}=(-1)^{n}\int_{0}^{\infty} rac{t^{n-1}e^{-tx}}{1-e^{-t}}dt$$ for $x>0$ and $n\geq2$, see~\cite[p.16]{MOS66} for instance. It follows from $a>b>0$ that for further simplification denote $u=k_0-(n_0+1)b/a$, $p=a/b$, and $q=a/\overline{b}$. Clearly, $ rac{1}{p}+ rac{1}{q}\leq1$. So one can deduce that  egin{eqnarray} (-1)^n[g(x)]^{(n)} &=&\int_{0}^{\infty}a^nt^{n-1}e^{-t(n_{0}+ax+1)}h(t,u)dt, \end{eqnarray} where $$h(t,u)= rac{1}{1-e^{-t}}- rac{e^{-tp(u+1)}}{1-e^{-pt}}- rac{e^{uqt}}{1-e^{-qt}}.$$ Furthermore, one can obtain the next claim for $-1\leq k_0-(n_0+1)b/a\leq0$.  egin{cl} If $-1\leq u\leq 0$, then $h(t,u)>0$. \end{cl} 	extbf{Proof of Claim:} It is obvious that $h(t, u)$ is concave in $u$. Thus it suffices to show $h(t, u)> 0$ for $u =-1$ and $u = 0$. Setting $u = 0$ since the case $u =-1$ can be obtained by switching the roles of $p$ and $q$, one has  egin{eqnarray*} h(t,0)&=& rac{e^{-t}}{1-e^{-t}}- rac{e^{-tp}}{1-e^{-pt}}- rac{e^{-qt}}{1-e^{-qt}}. \end{eqnarray*} Noting for $s>0$ that function $$f(s)= rac{se^{-s}}{1-e^{-s}}$$ strictly decreases in $s$ and $ rac{1}{p}+ rac{1}{q}\leq1$, one gets that  egin{eqnarray*} h(t,0)&\geq&( rac{1}{p}+ rac{1}{q}) rac{e^{-t}}{1-e^{-t}}- rac{e^{-tp}}{1-e^{-pt}}- rac{e^{-qt}}{1-e^{-qt}}\ &=& rac{f(t)-f(tp)}{tp}+ rac{f(t)-f(tq)}{tq}\ &\ge&0. \end{eqnarray*} This completes the proof of this Claim. Thus, by (ef{eqq}) and this Claim, one has $(-1)^n[g(x)]^{(n)}>0$, which implies that $(\log G(x))''$ is a completely monotonic function. This completes the proof.

[Proof Sketch] It is easy to observe that $ orall\mathrm{WMSO}
ot\leq\mathrm{MSO}$: consider the sentence $ orall X\exists y\, 
eg Xy$. This $ orall\mathrm{WMSO}$ sentence is true in a model iff the domain of the model is infinite. A straightforward monadic second-order Ehrenfeucht-Fra"{i}ss'{e} game argument can be used to establish that infinity is not expressible by any $\mathrm{MSO}$ sentence. To show that $ orall\mathrm{MSO}
ot\leq\mathrm{WMSO}$, consider the structures $(\mathbb{R},<)$ and $(\mathbb{Q},<)$. The structures can be separated by a sentence of $ orall\mathrm{MSO}$ stating that every subset bounded from above has a least upper bound. To see that the two structures cannot be separated by any sentence of $\mathrm{WMSO}$, consider the variant of the $\mathrm{MSO}$ Ehrenfeucht-Fra"{i}ss'{e} game where the players choose \emph{finite sets} in addition to domain elements. It is easy to establish that this game characterizes the expressivity of $\mathrm{WMSO}$. To see that the duplicator has a winning strategy in a game of any finite length played on the structures $(\mathbb{R},<)$ and $(\mathbb{Q},<)$, we devise an extension of the folklore winning strategy in the corresponding first-order game. Firstly, the duplicator can obviously always pick an element whose betweenness configuration corresponds exactly to that of the element picked by the spoiler. Furthermore, even if the spoiler picks a finite set, it is easy to see that the duplicator can pick his set such that each of its elements respect the betweenness configuration of the set picked by the spoiler.

As an auxiliary set of kernels, we define the Shannon-type kernels $\Phi_N^{Sh}$ and $	ilde{\Psi}_N^{Sh}$ via the symbols  egin{align}  ig(\Phi_N^{Sh} ig)^\wedge(n)=\left\{ egin{array}{ll} rac{1}{\sigma_n},&n\leq N,\0,&	extnormal{else},\end{array}ight.\qquad  ig(	ilde{\Phi}_{N}^{Sh} ig)^\wedge(n)=\left\{ egin{array}{ll}1,&n\leq \lfloor\kappa Nfloor,\0,&	extnormal{else},\end{array}ight. \end{align} and $ ig(	ilde{\Psi}_N^{Sh} ig)^\wedge(n)= ig(	ilde{\Phi}_N^{Sh} ig)^\wedge(n)- ig(\Phi_N^{Sh} ig)^\wedge(n)\sigma_n$. $\Phi_N^{Sh}$ represents the kernel of the so-called truncated singular value decomposition for the downward continuation operator ${T}^{down}$. By using the addition theorem for spherical harmonics and properties of the Legendre polynomials, we obtain  egin{align} \mathcal{F}(\Phi_N^{Sh},	ilde{\Psi}_N^{Sh})&= eta_N\sum_{n=0}^{N} rac{1}{\sigma_n^2}+8\pi^2 r^4 ig\|	ilde{\Psi}_N^{Sh} ig\|^2_{L^2([-1,1-ho])} \&\leq  eta_N rac{1-\left( rac{R}{r}ight)^{2(N+1)}}{1-\left( rac{R}{r}ight)^{2}}+8\pi^2\sum_{n=0}^{\lfloor\kappa Nfloor}\left( rac{2n+1}{4\pi}ight)^2\left\|P_night\|^2_{L^2([-1,1])}
onumber \&=  eta_N rac{1-\left( rac{R}{r}ight)^{2(N+1)}}{1-\left( rac{R}{r}ight)^{2}}+(\lfloor\kappa Nfloor+1)^2.
onumber \end{align} The kernels that minimize $\mathcal{F}$ are denoted by $\Phi_N$ and $	ilde{\Psi}_N$. In consequence,  egin{align} 	ilde{\alpha}_{N,n} ig(1-	ilde{\Phi}_{N}^\wedge(n) ig)^2&\leq\mathcal{F}(\Phi_N,	ilde{\Psi}_N)\leq \mathcal{F}(\Phi_N^{Sh},	ilde{\Psi}_N^{Sh}) \&\leq  eta_N rac{1-\left( rac{R}{r}ight)^{2(N+1)}}{1-\left( rac{R}{r}ight)^{2}}+(\lfloor\kappa Nfloor+1)^2,
onumber \end{align} for all $n\leq \lfloor\kappa Nfloor$. In combination with \eqref{eqn:ass1}, this leads to  egin{align}  ig|1-	ilde{\Phi}_{N}^\wedge(n) ig|= \mathcal{O}(N^{-1-\delta}), \end{align} uniformly for all $n\leq \lfloor\kappa Nfloor$ and $N	o\infty$. The estimate $|1-\Phi_N^\wedge(n)\sigma_n|=\mathcal{O}(N^{-1-\delta})$ follows in the same manner and holds uniformly for all $n\leq N$ and $N	o\infty$. Finally, since $	ilde{\Phi}_{N}^\wedge(n)=0$ for $n> \lfloor\kappa Nfloor$, we end up with  egin{align} \lim_{N	o\infty}\sup_{n=0,1,\ldots} rac{|1-	ilde{\Phi}_{N}^\wedge(n)|}{(n+ rac{1}{2})^{s}}=0, \end{align} which shows that the first term on the right hand side of the error estimate \eqref{eqn:errorestfinal} vanishes. The second term can be treated analogously. Due to the uniform boundedness of $|\Phi_J^\wedge(n)\sigma_n|$, there exists some constant $C>0$ such that \eqref{eqn:propx} implies  egin{align} \lim_{N	o\infty}\eps_1\sup_{n=0,1,\ldots}\left|\Phi_N^\wedge(n)ight|\leq C\lim_{N	o\infty}\eps_1\left( rac{R}{r}ight)^{N}=0. \end{align} so that the third term on the right hand side of \eqref{eqn:errorestfinal} vanishes as well. The fourth term does not vanish. However, taking a closer look at the derivation of this term, we see that it suffices to show that $ ig\|\sum_{n=0}^\infty\sum_{k=1}^{2n+1}	ilde{\Psi}_{N}^\wedge(n)F_r^\wedge(n,k) rac{1}{r}Y_{n,k} ig\|_{L^2(\Omega_r)}$ tends to zero (where $F$ stands for $U^+$ or $E_2$). Using the previous results and $|	ilde{\Psi}_N^\wedge(n)|\leq |1-{\Phi}_N^\wedge(n)\sigma_n|+|1-	ilde{\Phi}_N^\wedge(n)|$, it follows that  egin{align} \left\|\sum_{n=0}^\infty\sum_{k=1}^{2n+1}	ilde{\Psi}_{N}^\wedge(n)F_r^\wedge(n,k) rac{1}{r}Y_{n,k}ight\|_{L^2(\Omega_r)}^2\leq \mathcal{O}(N^{-2\delta})+C\sum_{n=N+1}^{\lfloor \kappa Nfloor}\sum_{k=1}^{2n+1}\left|F_r^\wedge(n,k)ight|^2, \end{align} for some constant $C>0$. Latter vanishes for $N	o\infty$ since $F$ (i.e., $U^+$ or $E_2$) is of class $L^2(\Omega_r)$. In order to handle the last term of the error estimate \eqref{eqn:errorestfinal}, it has to be shown that $\|	ilde{\Psi}_N\|_{L^2([-1,1-ho])}$ tends to zero. This, again, is generally not true. But, taking a closer look at the derivation of the estimate indicates that it suffices to show that \small egin{align} \Bigg\|\,\,\int_{\Omega_r\setminus\mathcal{C}_r(ho,\cdot)}	ilde{\Psi}_{N}(\cdot,y) U^+(y)d\omega(y)\Bigg\|_{L^2(\Omega_r)}, \qquad \eps_2\Bigg\|\,\,\int_{\Omega_r\setminus\mathcal{C}_r(ho,\cdot)}	ilde{\Psi}_{N}(\cdot,y) E_2(y)d\omega(y)\Bigg\|_{L^2(\Omega_r)} \end{align}
ormalsize vanish as $N	o\infty$. For the left expression we obtain  egin{equation}  egin{aligned} &\Bigg\|\,\,\int_{\Omega_r\setminus\mathcal{C}_r(ho,\cdot)}	ilde{\Psi}_{N}(\cdot,y) U^+(y)d\omega(y)\Bigg\|_{L^2(\Omega_r)} \&\leq\Bigg\|\,\,\int_{\Omega_r\setminus\mathcal{C}_r(ho,\cdot)}	ilde{\Psi}^{Sh}_{N}(\cdot,y) U^+(y)d\omega(y)\Bigg\|_{L^2(\Omega_r)} \&\quad\,+\Bigg\|\,\,\int_{\Omega_r\setminus\mathcal{C}_r(ho,\cdot)}\left(	ilde{\Psi}^{Sh}_{N}(\cdot,y)-	ilde{\Psi}_{N}(\cdot,y)ight) U^+(y)d\omega(y)\Bigg\|_{L^2(\Omega_r)} \&\leq\Bigg\|\,\,\int_{\Omega_r\setminus\mathcal{C}_r(ho,\cdot)}	ilde{\Psi}^{Sh}_{N}(\cdot,y) U^+(y)d\omega(y)\Bigg\|_{L^2(\Omega_r)} \&\quad\,+2\pi r^2\|	ilde{\Psi}^{Sh}_{N}-	ilde{\Psi}_{N}\|_{L^2([-1,1-ho])} \|U^+\|_{L^1(\Omega_r)}, \end{aligned} \end{equation} where Young's inequality has been used in the last row. Since $U^+\in \mathcal{H}_s(\Omega_r)$, $s\geq 2$, Proposition ef{prop:loc1} implies that the first term on the right hand side of \eqref{eqn:locest} tends to zero as $N	o\infty$. The second term on the right hand side of \eqref{eqn:locest} can be treated as follows  egin{equation}  egin{aligned} & ig\|	ilde{\Psi}^{Sh}_{N}-	ilde{\Psi}_{N} ig\|_{L^2([-1,1-ho])}^2 \&\leq\sum_{n=0}^{\lfloor\kappa Nfloor}\left( rac{2n+1}{4\pi}ight)^2\left|	ilde{\Psi}_N^\wedge(n)-\left(	ilde{\Psi}^{Sh}_Night)^\wedge(n)ight|^2\|P_n\|_{L^2([-1,1])}^2 \&\leq\sum_{n=0}^{\lfloor\kappa Nfloor} rac{2n+1}{8\pi^2}\left|\left|	ilde{\Phi}_{N}^\wedge(n)-1ight|+\left|{\Phi}_N^\wedge(n)\sigma_n-1ight|\chi_{[0,N]}(n)ight|^2 \&=\sum_{n=0}^{\lfloor\kappa Nfloor} rac{2n+1}{8\pi^2}\mathcal{O}\left(N^{-2(1+\delta)}ight)=\mathcal{O}\left(N^{-2\delta}ight). \end{aligned} \end{equation} By $\chi_{[0,N]}(n)$ we mean the characteristic function, i.e., it is equal to 1 if $n=0,\ldots,N$, and equal to 0 otherwise. In conclusion, the left expression in \eqref{eqn:altterms} vanishes as $N	o\infty$. For the right expression in \eqref{eqn:altterms}, we obtain the de