e0$ only for $i=1,2$, $\mu_2\in\{\la_1,\la_2\}$, but $\mu_2=\la_2$ contradicts $\la_1
ot>\la_2$, so that $\mu_2=\la_1$. The minimality of $\la_3$ implies $\mu_1=\la_3$ by Lemma ef{C Lem min}(i). We determine $A(\la_4)$ and $A(\la_2)$. By Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oii), we see that  egin{align} A(\la_4) e_1 \cong P(\mu_1)_{\la_4} \cong \D(\la_4), \quad A(\la_4) e_2 \cong P(\mu_2)_{\la_4} =0. \end{align} As $A (\la_4)$ is a two-sided-ideal and $\D(\la_4) \cong L(\mu_1)$, \eqref{F DB 2} implies that $A(\la_4) = K \g^3$. In a similar way, we see that  egin{align}  egin{split} &(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4) )e_1 \cong P(\mu_1)_{\la_2} / P(\mu_1)_{\la_4} \cong \D(\la_2), \ &(A(\la_2) / A(\la_4)) e_2 \cong P(\mu_2)_{\la_2} / P(\mu_2)_{\la_4} \cong \D(\la_2). \end{split} \end{align} Note that $A (\la_2)$ is a two-sided-ideal and $[ \D(\la_2) ] = [L(\mu_1)] + [L(\mu_2)]$. Then, since $\dim e_1(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4) )e_1 = 1$, there is a nonzero element $ae_1+b\g+c\g^2$ in $A(\la_2)e_1$. If $a
e0$ then $a\g+b\g^2 \in A(\la_2)e_1$, which implies $\dim e_1(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4) )e_1>1$. Thus, $a=0$. The same argument shows $b=0$ and we conclude that $\g^2\in A(\la_2)e_1$. On the other hand, $\dim e_2(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4) )e_1 = 1$ implies that there is a nonzero element $a _1+b _1\g$ in $A(\la_2)e_1$. We may also prove $ eta_1\gamma\alpha_1 \in A(\la_2)e_2$ and $\gamma\alpha_1 \in A(\la_2)e_2$ in the similar manner. Hence,  egin{align*} &(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4)) e_1 \equiv K \g^2 \oplus K (a _1 + b _1\g) \mod A (\la_4), \ &(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4))e_2 \equiv K \g \a_1 \oplus K  _1 \g \a_1 \mod A (\la_4). \end{align*} We observe that $\a_1$ acts as zero on $(A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4))e_2$. Then, $\a_1(a _1 + b _1\g) \equiv a\g^2$ implies $a=0$. However, if $a=0$ then we meet another contradiction  egin{align*} &\Soc \D(\la_2) \cong \Soc (A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4)) e_1 \cong L(\mu_1) \oplus L(\mu_2), \ &\Soc \D(\la_2) \cong \Soc (A(\la_2)/ A(\la_4))e_2 \cong L(\mu_2). \end{align*} We have proved that $A$ is not cellular.

Suppose that $K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}$ is cellular. If $\ol{Q}$ has a multiple edge, then $Q$ contains a subquiver \[ \xymatrix{Q' = i \ar[rr]<-1.5ex> \ar[rr]<1.8ex> & \vdots & j } \] with $k$ arrows ($k \geq 2$) by Proposition ef{C Prop simple}. By the defining relations, we see that $(e_i + e_j)(K\wh{Q} / I_{\wh{Q}})(e_i + e_j) \cong K \wh{Q'} / I_{\wh{Q'}}$, and its Cartan matrix is \[  C'= egin{pmatrix} 2 & k \ k & 2 \end{pmatrix}. \] By Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oiv), $(e_i + e_j)(K\wh{Q} / I_{\wh{Q}})(e_i + e_j)$ is not cellular since $\det  C' \leq 0$. Then, by Lemma ef{C Lem idem}, $K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}$ is not cellular, contradicting our assumption. Hence $\ol{Q}$ has no multiple edge. Note that $Q$ does not have an oriented cycle since $Q$ is acyclic. In particular the underlying graph of $Q$ does not contain a loop. Suppose that $Q$ contains a subquiver $Q^{\sharp}$ whose underlying graph is a cycle of length $l\ge2$. We label its vertices as follows.  egin{align*} \xymatrix@R=3pt{ &i_2 \ar@{-}[r] & i_3 \ar@{-}[dr] & \ &&& \ar@{.}[dd] \ \ol{Q^{\sharp}} = i_1\ar@{-}[uur] &&& & \ &&& \ar@{-}[dl] \ & i_l \ar@{-}[uul]& i_{l-1} \ar@{-}[l] & } \end{align*} Then we see that $(e_{i_1} + \dots + e_{i_l}) (K\wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}) (e_{i_1} + \dots + e_{i_l}) \cong K \wh{Q^{\sharp}}/ I_{\wh{Q^{\sharp}}}$, and its Cartan matrix is  egin{align*}  C^{\sharp} =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \ &&&&& \vdots &&&& \ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*} Suppose that $K \wh{Q^{\sharp}}/ I_{\wh{Q^{\sharp}}}$ is a cellular algebra with cell datum $(\vL,\cT, \cC, \iota)$, and let $ D^{\sharp}$ be its decomposition matrix. Then, by Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oiii), we have  egin{align*}  D^{\sharp} =  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 1 \ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 0 \ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 & 0 \ &&&& \vdots &&& \ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 1 & 1 & 0 \ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*} modulo rearrangement of rows. Put $\vL =\{ \la_1, \la_2,\dots, \la_{l}\}$ such that $([\D(\la_i) : L(\la_j)])_{1\leq i,j \leq l} =  D^{\sharp}$. Then, by Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oi), we have $\la_l < \la_1 < \la_2 < \dots < \la_{l-1} < \la_l$ in the partial order, which is impossible. Hence, $(e_{i_1} + \dots + e_{i_l}) (K\wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}) (e_{i_1} + \dots + e_{i_l})$ is not cellular, and $K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}$ is not cellular by Lemma ef{C Lem idem}, contradicting our assumption again. Assume that $\ol{Q}$ has no multiple edge and no cycle. If $\ol{Q}$ has a branch, $Q$ contains a subquiver $Q^{
atural}$ whose underlying graph is of the following form  egin{align*} &\xymatrix@R=5pt{ & & i_3 \ \ol{Q^{
atural}} = i_1 \ar@{-}[r] & i_2 \ar@{-}[ur] \ar@{-}[dr] & & \ && i_4} \end{align*} We see that $(e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4}) K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}} (e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4}) \cong K \wh{Q^{
atural}}/ I_{\wh{Q^{
atural}}}$, and its Cartan matrix is  egin{align*}  C^{
atural} =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*} However, there is no matrix $ D$ with non-negative integral entries that satisfies $ D^T  D =  C$. Thus, $(e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4}) K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}} (e_{i_1}+ \dots + e_{i_4})$ is not cellular by Proposition ef{C Prop decom matrix} (oiii), and $K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}$ is not cellular by Lemma ef{C Lem idem}. Now the only graph $\ol{Q}$ without cycles or loops, and branch points, is a straight line. Hence we have proved that $\ol{Q}$ is a straight line with no multiple edge if $K \wh{Q} / I_{\wh{Q}}$ is cellular. Conversely, suppose that $\ol{Q}$ is a straight line with no multiple edge. Then we easily see that $K \wh{Q}/ I_{\wh{Q}}$ is isomorphic to the Brauer tree algebra whose Brauer tree is a straight line with no exceptional vertex, which is cellular by Proposition ef{F Prop BT}.

Recall the self-map digraph $\G^{\G}$, with vertices the self-maps of $\G$, and an edge $(f,g) \in E(\G^\G)$ between self-maps $f$ and $g$ if and only if for every edge $(x,y) \in E(\G)$, we have that $(f(x),g(y)) \in E(\G)$. In the self-map digraph, the endomorphisms are precisely the looped vertices. Consider the homomorphism $\phi: \H^n ightarrow \H^{\H}$ induced by an $n+1$-ary polymorphism of $\H$ where $n \geq 1$; suppose for a contradiction that the polymorphism depends on all its variables. Then clearly the image of $\phi$ contains at least two elements, and at least one of these elements is not a constant map. 
oindent{ f Claim 0.} {\em The only loops in $\H^{\H}$ are the constant maps 1 and 2 and the identity. } 
oindent {\em Proof of Claim 0}: let $f$ be an endomorphism of $\H$. Then $f$ maps loops to loops. Since $(1,2)\in E(\H)$, $f$ can either map both to 1, both to 2 , or fix both. Clearly if it fixes both $f$ is the identity. Otherwise, if $f(1)=f(2)=i$, then $(f(0),i) , (i,f(0)) \in E(\H)$ so $f(0)=i$. Without fear of confusion, we denote the constant maps by 0, 1 and 2. We denote the identity map by $id$. 
oindent{ f Claim 1.} {\em (a) There are no edges in $\H^{\H}$ between the identity and the constant maps; (b) if $(2,f), (f,1) \in E(\H^\H)$, then $f=0$; (c) if $c$ is a constant map such that $(f,c),(c,f) \in E(\H^\H)$ then $f = c$. } 
oindent {\em Proof of Claim 1}: $(2,f) \in E(\H^\H)$ implies $(2,f(i)) \in E(\H)$ for all $i$ so $f(i) \in \{0,2\}$ for all $i$. Similarly $(f,1)\in E(\H^\H)$ implies $f(i) \in \{0,1\}$ for all $i$ so (b) follows immediately. To prove (a), observe that any in- or out-neighbour of a constant cannot be surjective since no constant has in- or out-neighbourhood of size 3. For (c), argue again as we just did: $(1,f) \in E(\H^\H)$ means $f(i) \in \{1,2\}$ for all $i$, and $(f,1)\in E(\H^\H)$ implies $f(i) \in \{0,1\}$ for all $i$ and hence $f=1$; the proof for $c=2$ is identical. 
oindent{ f Claim 2.} {\em If $(f,id),(id,f) \in E(\H^\H)$ then $f = id$. } 
oindent {\em Proof of Claim 2}: Assume that $(f,id),(id,f) \in E(\H^\H)$. If $i$ is a loop then $(i,i)\in E(\H)$ implies $(f(i),i),(i,f(i))\in E(\H)$ thus $f(i) = i$. Now $(0,1)\in E(\H)$ implies $(f(0),1)\in E(\H)$, and $(2,0)\in E(\H)$ implies $(2,f(0))\in E(\H)$ so $f(0)=0$. Obviously the loops of $\H^n$ are precisely the tuples whose coordinates belong to $\{1,2\}$. Call the subdigraph induced by these vertices $\W$, and notice it is weakly connected. Then the homomorphism $\phi$ must map $\W$ onto a weakly connected digraph consisting of loops only. By Claim 1 (a) either (i) $\W$ is entirely mapped to $\{id\}$ or (ii) $\W$ is mapped by $\phi$ to the set $\{1,2\}$. Choose any tuple $X$ of $\H^n$ containing a coordinate equal to 0. Then there exist $Y, Z \in W$ such that $(X,Z), (Z,Y), (Y,X) \in E(\H^n)$: indeed, let $Z$ be obtained from $X$ by replacing each 0 entry by 1, fixing all other coordinates, and let $Y$ be obtained from $X$ by replacing each 0 entry by 2, fixing all other coordinates. Thus in case (i) we get that $id = \phi(Y), (\phi(Y),\phi(X)),(\phi(X),\phi(Z)) \in E(\H^\H)$ and $\phi(Z)=id$ so by Claim 2 $\phi$ maps all of $\H^n$ to $id$. In case (ii), we get that $\phi(Y)$ and $\phi(Z)$ belong to $\{1,2\}$; since $(\phi(Z),\phi(Y))\in E(\H^\H)$ we must in fact have that $(\phi(Z),\phi(Y))$ belongs to $\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,2)\}$; thus we get one of the following cases, namely $(1,\phi(X)),(\phi(X),1) \in E(\H^\H)$ or $(1,\phi(X)),(\phi(X),2) \in E(\H^\H)$ or $(2,\phi(X)),(\phi(X),2) \in E(\H^\H)$. By Claim 1 (b) and (c) $\phi(X)$ must be one of the constant maps 0, 1 or 2. Thus we conclude that $\phi$ either maps all of $\H^n$ to $\{id\}$, in which case our polymorphism cannot depend on its first $n$ variables; or otherwise $\phi$ maps all of $\H^n$ to constant maps, and then the polymorphism does not depend on its last variable, also a contradiction.

Consider the least squares problem \eqref{eq:lsq} with $\y = \eps$:  egin{equation*} \min_{\Pi,  eta} 
norm{\eps - \Pi X  eta}_2^2. \end{equation*} Omitting the term not depending on $ eta$, an equivalent problem is given by  egin{equation*} \min_{\Pi,  eta} -2 \scp{\Pi \eps}{X  eta} + 
norm{X  eta}_2^2. \end{equation*} Re-parameterizing $X  eta = u \cdot r$, with $r \geq 0$ for $u \in 	ext{range}(X) \cap \mathbb{S}^{n-1}$, we obtain the optimization problem  egin{equation*} \min_{\Pi, u \in 	ext{range}(X) \cap \mathbb{S}^{n-1}, r \geq 0} -2 \scp{\Pi \eps}{u} r + r^2. \end{equation*} Observe that for any $u$ and $\Pi$, the optimal value of $r$ is found as  egin{equation*} \wh{r}(u) = \max\{\scp{\Pi \eps}{u},0 \}. \end{equation*} In particular, for a minimizer $(\wh{\Pi}, \wh{u})$,  egin{equation*} \wh{r} \coloneq \wh{r}(\wh{\Pi}, \wh{u}) = 
scp{\wh{\Pi} \eps}{\wh{u}} \end{equation*} so that $\wh{r} = 
norm{X \wh{ eta}}_2$, and the optimal value of the objective is given by $-\wh{r}^2$. We hence have  egin{align*} \wh{r} &= \max_{u \in 	ext{range}(X) \cap \mathbb{S}^{n-1}, \Pi} 
scp{\Pi \eps}{u} \ &\geq \max_{1 \leq j \leq d, \Pi} 
scp{\Pi \eps}{X_j / 
norm{X_j}_2} \ &\geq \max_{\Pi} 
scp{\Pi \eps}{X_1}  rac{1}{
norm{X_1}_2}. \end{align*} We now bound  egin{equation*} \max_{\Pi} 
scp{\Pi \eps}{X_1} \geq 
scp{\Pi_{\pm} \eps}{X_1}, \end{equation*} where the permutation $\Pi_{\pm}$ is defined as follows. Define  egin{align*} n_{\eps,+} = \{i:\; \epsilon_i > 0\}, \quad n_{\eps,-} = n - n_{\eps,+}, \quad n_{X_1,+} = \{i:\; X_{i1} > 0\}, \quad n_{X_1,-} = n - n_{X_1,+}. \end{align*} Let further  egin{equation} n_+ = \min\{n_{\eps,+}, n_{X_1,+} \}, \quad n_- = \min\{n_{\eps,-}, n_{X_1,-} \}, \quad n_{\pm} = n - n_+ - n_-. \end{equation} Denote by  egin{alignat*}{2} &i_1^+ < \ldots < i_{n_{X,+}}^+ \quad &&	ext{the indices of the positive entries of $X_1$},\ &j_1^+ < \ldots < j_{n_{\eps,+}}^+ \quad &&	ext{the indices of the positive entries of $\eps$},\ &i_1^- < \ldots < i_{n_{X,-}}^- \quad &&	ext{the indices of the negative entries of $X_1$},\ &j_1^- < \ldots < j_{n_{\eps,-}}^- \quad &&	ext{the indices of the negative entries of $\eps$}. \end{alignat*} Furthermore, let  egin{align*} &i_{1}^{\pm} < \ldots < i_{n_{\pm}}^{\pm} \ &j_{1}^{\pm} < \ldots < j_{n_{\pm}}^{\pm} \end{align*} be the indices in  egin{align*} & \{1,\ldots,n\} \setminus (\{i_1^+,\ldots, i_{n_+}^+\} \cup \{i_1^-,\ldots, i_{n_-}^-\}) \ 	ext{respectively}\;\, & \{1,\ldots,n\} \setminus (\{j_1^+,\ldots, j_{n_+}^+\} \cup \{j_1^-,\ldots, j_{n_-}^-\}) \end{align*} in increasing order. We then construct $\Pi_{\pm}$ such that  egin{equation*} 
scp{\Pi_{\pm} \eps}{X_1} = \sum_{k = 1}^{n_+} X_{1{i_k^+}} \epsilon_{j_k^+} + \sum_{k = 1}^{n_-} X_{1{i_k^-}} \epsilon_{j_k^-} + \sum_{k = 1}^{n_{\pm}} X_{1{i_k}^{\pm}} \; \epsilon_{{j_k}^{\pm}}. \end{equation*} All terms inside the first two sums are i.i.d.~from the same distribution as $|g| |h|$, where $g$ and $h$ are independent $N(0,1)$-random variables, and accordingly the terms inside the last summand are i.i.d.~from the same distribution as $-|g||h|$. Conditional on $n_{\pm}$, hence $
scp{\Pi_{\pm} \eps}{X_1}$ follows the same distribution as  egin{equation*} \sum_{i = 1}^{n - n_{\pm}} |g_i| |h_i| - \sum_{i=n - n_{\pm} + 1}^{n} |g_i| |h_i|, \end{equation*} where $\{ g_i \}_{i = 1}^n$ and $\{ h_i \}_{i = 1}^n$ are two independent sequences of $n$ i.i.d.~random variables from the $N(0,1)$-distribution. The expectation of the above expression (conditional on $n_{\pm}$) is given by  egin{equation*} (n - 2 n_{\pm}) \E[|g||h|] = (n - 2 n_{\pm}) \E[|g|]^2 = (n - 2 n_{\pm})  rac{2}{\pi}. \end{equation*} From Proposition 5.16 in \cite{Vershynin2010}, we have the following concentration inequality:  egin{equation*} \p \left(\left|\sum_{i = 1}^{n - n_{\pm}} (|g_i| |h_i| - \E[|g||h|]) - \sum_{i=n - n_{\pm} + 1}^{n} (|g_i| |h_i| - \E[|g||h|]) ight| \geq (n - 2 n_{\pm})  rac{1}{\pi} \Big| n_{\pm} ight) \leq \exp(-n c). \end{equation*} Hence, conditional on $n_{\pm}$, with probability at least $1 - \exp(-nc)$,  egin{equation*} \sum_{i = 1}^{n - n_{\pm}} |g_i| |h_i| - \sum_{i=n - n_{\pm} + 1}^{n} |g_i| |h_i| \geq  rac{1}{\pi} (n - 2 n_{\pm}). \end{equation*} Consider the definition of $n_+$ and $n_-$ Observe that $n_{\eps, +}$, $n_{X_1, +}$, $n_{\eps, -}$ and $n_{X_1, -}$ are each binomial random variables with $n$ trials and probability of success $1/2$. By repeated application of Hoeffding's inequality, we hence have  egin{equation*} \p(n_{+} \leq 3n/8) \leq 2\exp(-n/32), \quad \p(n_{-} \leq 3n/8) \leq 2 \exp(-n/32). \end{equation*} Conditional on the events $\{ n_{+} > 3n/8\}$ and $\{ n_{-} > 3n/8\}$, we have $n_{\pm} \leq 1/4$ and thus with probability at least $1 - C \exp(-nc)$  egin{equation*} \sum_{i = 1}^{n - n_{\pm}} |g_i| |h_i| - \sum_{i=n - n_{\pm} + 1}^{n} |g_i| |h_i| \geq  rac{n}{2\pi} . \end{equation*} Accordingly, the same applies to the event $\{ 
scp{\Pi_{\pm} \eps}{X_1} >  rac{n}{2\pi} \}$. Lemma ef{lem:normconcentration} in Appendix ef{app:aux} yields $
norm{X_1}_2 \leq 2 \sqrt{n}$ with probability at least $1 - \exp(-n/2)$. Putting together the pieces, it follows that with probability at least $1 - C \exp(-nc)$  egin{equation*} 
norm{X \wh{ eta}}_2 = \wh{r} \geq  rac{\sqrt{n}}{4\pi} \quad \Rightarrow \; 
norm{\wh{ eta}}_2 \geq  rac{\sqrt{n}}{\sigma_{\max}(X) 4\pi}, \end{equation*} where $\sigma_{\max}(X)$ denotes the maximum singular value of $X$. Finally, Lemma ef{lem:extremesingularvalues} in Appendix ef{app:aux} yields that $\sigma_{\max}(X) \leq \sqrt{2n} + \sqrt{d}$ with probability at least $1 - \exp(-c n)$, which concludes the proof.

Equipped with Lemmas ef{lem:bound_pi} and ef{lem:Xteps}, we are in position to conclude Theorem ef{theo:constrained_permutation}. Expanding the expression for $\wh{ eta}$ in \eqref{eq:decomposition}, we obtain that  egin{align*} \wh{ eta} = \left( rac{X^{\T} X}{n} ight)^{-1}  rac{X^{\T} \wh{\Pi}^{\T} \y}{n} &= \left( rac{X^{\T} X}{n} ight)^{-1}  rac{X^{\T} \Pi^{*\T} \y}{n} + \left( rac{X^{\T} X}{n} ight)^{-1}  rac{X^{\T} (\wh{\Pi}^{\T} - \Pi^{*\T}) \y}{n} \ &=  eta^* + \left(  rac{X^{\T} X}{n} ight)^{-1}  rac{X^{\T} (\eps + (\wh{\Pi}^{\T} - \Pi^{*\T}) \y)}{n}, \end{align*} and thus  egin{align*} 
norm{\wh{ eta} -  eta^*}_2 &\leq  rac{
orm{ \left( rac{X^{\T} X}{n} ight)^{-1}  rac{X^{\T} \eps}{\sqrt{n}}}_2}{\sqrt{n}} +  rac{
orm{\left( rac{X^{\T} X}{n} ight)^{-1}  rac{X^{\T}}{\sqrt{n}}}_2 
norm{(\wh{\Pi}^{\T} - \Pi^{*\T}) \y}_2}{\sqrt{n}} \ &\leq  rac{
orm{ \left( rac{X^{\T} X}{n} ight)^{-1}  rac{X^{\T} \eps}{\sqrt{n}}}_2}{\sqrt{n}} +  rac{ rac{1}{\sigma_{\min}(X / \sqrt{n})}
norm{(\wh{\Pi}^{\T} - \Pi^{*\T}) \y}_2}{\sqrt{n}} \ &\leq  rac{\sigma}{1 - \sqrt{ rac{4 d \vee \log n}{n}}} \left(\sqrt{ rac{5 (d \vee \log n)}{n}} +  rac{2 (1 + \sqrt{2})\sigma \epss^{-2} \{w(\mc{T}) \vee \log n\}}{\sqrt{n}} ight) \end{align*} with the stated probability by combining Lemmas ef{lem:bound_pi} and ef{lem:Xteps}.

We first decompose  egin{equation*} \pr_X^{\perp} \y = \pr_X^{\perp} (X  eta^* + \sqrt{n} e^* + \eps) = \pr_X^{\perp} \sqrt{n} e^* + \wt{\eps}, \quad \wt{\eps} \coloneq \pr_X^{\perp} \eps. \end{equation*} According to Lemma ef{lem:decoupling_LAD}, since $\wt{e}$ is a minimizer, we have that  egin{equation*}  rac{1}{n} 
norm{\pr_X^{\perp} \sqrt{n} (e^* - \wt{e}) + \wt{\eps}}_2^2 + \lambda 
norm{\wt{e}}_1 \leq  rac{1}{n} 
norm{\wt{\eps}}_2^2 + \lambda 
norm{e^*}_1. \end{equation*} Expanding the square on the left-hand side and re-arranging terms, we obtain that  egin{equation}  rac{1}{n} 
norm{\pr_X^{\perp} \sqrt{n} (e^* - \wt{e})}_2^2 + \lambda 
