To calculate the probability of interest, we begin by analyzing a simplified experiment. Suppose there are $b$ range items $r_1, \dots, r_b$ each appearing greater than $T$ times in $x$. We compute the probability that \emph{none} of the items $r_1, \dots, r_b$ appear in the sample $x_\Scal$. If $T \ge N - S$, then this probability is zero. Otherwise, by direct calculation, we have for each $i = 1, \dots, b$: \[\Pr_{\Scal}[r_i 
otin x_\Scal | r_1 
otin x_\Scal, \dots, r_{i-1} 
otin x_\Scal] \le \prod_{j = 0}^{S-1} \left(1 -  rac{T}{N-j} ight) \le \left( 1 -  rac{T}{N}ight)^S \le \exp \left(- rac{ST}{N} ight).\] Hence the probability that none of $r_1, \dots, r_b$ appear is \[\Pr_{\Scal}[r_1 
otin x_\Scal \land \dots \land r_b 
otin x_\Scal] = \prod_{i = 1}^b \Pr_{\Scal}[r_i 
otin x_\Scal | r_1 
otin x_\Scal, \dots, r_{i-1} 
otin x_\Scal] \le \exp \left(- rac{bST}{N} ight).\] Now fix an arbitrary $x$, and let $r_1, \dots, r_{k}$ be the range items appearing greater than $T$ times in $x$. Observe that $k \le N/T$. We estimate the probability of interest by taking a union bound over all subsets of $r_1, \dots, r_k$ of size $b$:  egin{align*} \Pr_{\Scal}[b_{\Rcal(x_\Scal)}(x) \ge b] &\le \sum_{Y \subseteq [k] : |Y| = b} \Pr_{\Scal}[r_{i_j} 
otin x_\Scal \quad  orall j \in Y] \ & \le  inom{k}{b} \exp \left(- rac{bST}{N}ight) \ &\le \exp \left( - rac{bST}{N} + b \log Night). \end{align*}

By renormalizing, it suffices to construction a function $\omega : [T]_0 	o \R$ such that  egin{align} &\omega(0) - \sum_{t = 1}^T \omega(t) \ge (1 - \delta)\|\omega\|_1 \ & 	ext{For all univariate polynomials } q \colon \R 	o \R	ext{, } \deg q < c_1\sqrt{\delta T} \implies \sum_{t=0}^T \omega(t) \cdot q(t) = 0 \ & |\omega(t)| \le  rac{170 \exp(-c_2 t \sqrt{\delta} /\sqrt{T})\|\omega\|_1}{\delta \cdot t^2} \qquad  orall t = 1, \dots, T.\end{align} Let $c = \lceil 8 / \delta ceil$ below. We will freely use the fact that since $\delta \le 1/2$, we have $c \le c^2 / (c-1) \le 10 /\delta$. Let $m = \lfloor \sqrt{T/2c} floor$ and define the set \[S = \{1, c\} \cup \{2ci^2 : 0 \le i \le m\}.\] Note that $|S| \ge c_1 \sqrt{\delta T}$ for some absolute constant $c_1 > 0$. Define the function \[\omega(t) =  rac{(-1)^{t+(T-m)}}{T!}  inom{T}{t} \prod_{r \in [T]_0 \setminus S} (t - r).\] Property~\eqref{eqn:or-sym-phd-ref} follows from the following well-known combinatorial identity.  egin{fact}[e.g., \cite{concrete} Equation (5.23) or \cite{osnewbounds}] Let $T \in \N$, and let $p$ be a polynomial of degree less than $T$. Then \[\sum_{t = 0}^T (-1)^t  inom{T}{t} p(t) = 0.\] \end{fact} Expanding out the binomial coefficient in the definition of $\omega$ reveals that \[|\omega(t)| =  egin{cases} \prod\limits_{r \in S \setminus \{t\}}  rac{1}{|t - r|} & 	ext{ for } t \in S, \ 0 & 	ext{ otherwise.} \end{cases}\] We now use this characterization to establish the improved tail decay property \eqref{eqn:or-sym-decay-ref}. This clearly holds for $t = 1$ with $c_2 = 1/10$, since $|\omega(1)| \le \|\omega\|_1$ and \[ rac{170}{\delta} \exp(-c_2\sqrt{\delta} / \sqrt{T}) \ge 340 \cdot e^{-\sqrt{2} / 10} > 1.\] For $t = c$, we have  egin{align}  rac{|\omega(c)|}{\omega(0)} &=  rac{c\prod_{i = 1}^m (2ci^2)}{c(c-1)\prod_{i = 1}^m (2ci^2 - c)} 
onumber \ &=  rac{1}{c-1}\left(\prod_{i = 1}^m  rac{i^2 - 1/2}{i^2}ight)^{-1} 
onumber \ &\le  rac{1}{c-1} \left(1 - \sum_{i=1}^m  rac{1}{2i^2}ight)^{-1} 
onumber \ &\le  rac{1}{c-1} \left(1 -  rac{\pi^2}{12}ight)^{-1} \le  rac{6}{c - 1} \end{align} where the first inequality follows from the fact that $\prod_{i = 1}^m (1 - a_i) \ge 1 - \sum_{i = 1}^m a_i$ for $a_i \in (0, 1)$. Now note that  egin{align*}  rac{|\omega(c)|}{\|\omega\|_1} &\le  rac{|\omega(c)|}{\omega(0)} \ &\le  rac{6}{c - 1} \ &\le  rac{60}{\delta \cdot c^2} & 	ext{ since } 10/\delta \ge c^2 / (c-1) \ & \le  rac{170}{\delta \cdot c^2} \cdot e^{-c\sqrt{\delta}/10\sqrt{T}}, \end{align*} since $\delta \ge 1/T$ and hence $e^{-c\sqrt{\delta}/10\sqrt{T}} \ge e^{-1}$. Thus~\eqref{eqn:or-sym-decay-ref} holds for $t = c$, recalling that $c_2 = 1/10$. For $t = 2cj^2$ with $j \ge 1$, we get  egin{align*}  rac{|\omega(t)|}{\omega(0)} &=  rac{c\prod_{i = 1}^m (2ci^2)}{(2cj^2 - 1)(2cj^2-c)\prod_{i \in [m]_0 \setminus \{j\}} |2ci^2 - 2cj^2|} \ &=  rac{c(m!)^2}{(4c^2j^4 - (2c^2 + 2c)j^2 + c)\prod_{i \in [m]_0 \setminus \{j\}} (i+j)|i-j|} \ &=  rac{c}{4c^2j^4 - (2c^2 + 2c)j^2 + c} \cdot  rac{(m!)^2}{(m+j)!(m-j)!}. \end{align*} For $j \ge 1$, the first factor is bounded by \[ rac{c}{4c^2j^4 - (2c^2 + 2c)j^2 + c} \le  rac{3c}{(2cj^2)^2},\] using the fact that $c \ge 2$. We control the second factor by  egin{align*}  rac{(m!)^2}{(m+j)!(m-j)!} &=  rac{m}{m+j} \cdot  rac{m-1}{m+j-1} \cdot \ldots \cdot  rac{m-j+1}{m+1} \ &\le \left( rac{m}{m+j} ight)^j \ &\le \left(1 -  rac{j}{2m} ight)^j \ &\le e^{-j^2/2m}, \end{align*} where the last inequality uses the fact that $1 - x \le e^{-x}$ for all $x$. Since \[ rac{|\omega(t)|}{\|\omega\|_1} \le  rac{|\omega(2cj^2)|}{\omega(0)} \le  rac{3c}{(2cj^2)^2} \cdot e^{-2cj^2/(4cm)} \le  rac{170}{\delta \cdot t^2} \cdot e^{-t \sqrt{\delta} / 10 \sqrt{T}},\] this establishes~\eqref{eqn:or-sym-decay-ref}. What remains is to perform the correlation calculation to establish~\eqref{eqn:or-sym-corr-ref}. For $t = 1$, we observe \[ rac{|\omega(1)|}{\omega(0)} =  rac{c\prod_{i = 1}^m (2ci^2)}{(c-1)\prod_{i = 1}^m (2ci^2 - 1)} \ge \prod_{i=1}^m  rac{i^2}{i^2 - 1/(2c)} \ge 1.\] Next, we observe that the total contribution of $t > c$ to $\|\omega\|_1/\omega(0)$ is at most  egin{equation} \sum_{t > c}  rac{|\omega(t)|}{\omega(0)} \le \sum_{j=1}^{m}  rac{3c}{(2cj^2)^2} < \sum_{j=1}^\infty  rac{3}{4cj^4} =  rac{\pi^4}{120 c}. \end{equation} Next, we calculate  egin{align} \omega(0) - \sum_{t = 1}^T \omega(t) &\ge \omega(0) - \omega(1) - \left( \sum_{t = c}^T |\omega(t)|ight) 
onumber \ &\ge \omega(0) - \omega(1) - \left( \omega(c) + \omega(0) \cdot  rac{\pi^4}{120c}ight) & 	ext{by } \eqref{eqn:omega-tail} 
onumber \ &\ge - \omega(1) + \omega(0) \left(1 -  rac{6}{c-1} -  rac{\pi^4}{120c}ight) & 	ext{by } \eqref{eqn:omega-2} 
onumber \ &\ge - \omega(1) + (1 - \delta)\omega(0) & 	ext{by our choice of } c \ge 8/\delta. \end{align} On the other hand,  egin{align} \|\omega\|_1 &\le \omega(0) - \omega(1) + \omega(2) + \omega(0) \cdot  rac{\pi^4}{120c} & 	ext{by } \eqref{eqn:omega-tail} 
onumber \ &\le -\omega(1) + \omega(0) \left(1 +  rac{6}{c - 1} +  rac{\pi^4}{120c} ight) &	ext{by } \eqref{eqn:omega-2}
onumber \ &\le -\omega(1) + (1 + \delta)\omega(0) & 	ext{since } c \ge 8/\delta. \end{align} Combining~\eqref{eqn:fudgethis} and~\eqref{eqn:fudgethat}, and using the fact that $-\omega(1) \ge \omega(0)$ shows that \[ rac{\omega(0) - \sum_{t = 1}^k \omega(t)}{\|\omega\|_1} \ge  rac{-\omega(1) + (1-\delta) \omega(0)}{-\omega(1) + (1+\delta) \omega(0)} \ge  rac{2-\delta}{2 + \delta} \ge 1 - \delta.\] This establishes~\eqref{eqn:or-sym-corr-ref}, completing the proof.

Let us exemplify how this works starting with \eqref{Stokes-comp-2nd} and computing the $S_{-k}^F$. For $n=1$, a power-series expansion of the left-hand side, together with the recursive action of the differential operator on the right-hand side, leads to  egin{eqnarray} \sum_{\ell=0}^{+\infty} \left(-S_{-1}^{Z}ight)^{\ell} 	au_{1}^{\ell+1} & = & 	au_{1} + \sum_{k_{1}=1}^{+\infty} S_{-k_{1}}^{F}\, 	au_{1}^{k_{1}+1} +  rac{1}{2!} \sum_{k_{1},k_{2}=1}^{+\infty} \left( k_{1}+1 ight) S_{-k_{1}}^{F} S_{-k_{2}}^{F}\, 	au_{1}^{k_{1}+k_{2}+1} + \ &+&  rac{1}{3!} \sum_{k_{1},k_{2},k_{3}=1}^{+\infty} \left( k_{1}+1 ight) \left( k_{1}+k_{2}+1 ight) S_{-k_{1}}^{F} S_{-k_{2}}^{F} S_{-k_{3}}^{F}\, 	au_{1}^{\sum_{i=1}^{3}k_{i}+1} + \cdots. 
onumber \end{eqnarray} 
oindent Let us compare equal powers of $	au_1^{\ell+1}$. For $\ell=0$ we get an identity; for the next few $\ell$'s we find  egin{eqnarray} \ell = 1: & -S_{-1}^{Z} & = S_{-1}^{F}, \ \ell = 2: & \left( -S_{-1}^{Z} ight)^{2} & = S_{-2}^{F} +  rac{1}{2!} \left( 1+1 ight) \left( S_{-1}^{F} ight)^{2}, \ \ell = 3: & \left( -S_{-1}^{Z} ight)^{3} & = S_{-3}^{F} +  rac{1}{2!} \left( 2+3 ight) S_{-1}^{F} S_{-2}^{F} +  rac{1}{3!} \left( 2 	imes 3 ight) \left( S_{-1}^{F} ight)^{3}. \end{eqnarray} 
oindent The first equation above, \eqref{Stokes-comp-one}, immediately yields $S_{-1}^{F} = -S_{-1}^{Z}$. Recursively solving the following equations one sees that \eqref{Stokes-comp-two} then yields $S_{-2}^{F} =0$ and \eqref{Stokes-comp-three} $S_{-3}^{F} =0$. This is a pattern which generalizes to higher powers of $	au_1$. Indeed, the first time the Stokes constant $S_{-k}^F$ will enter the game is when we write down the equation associated to $\ell=k$. But, at this stage, such equation will also include a term just in $\left(S_{-1}^Fight)^k$, while all other terms will be products of Stokes constants which always include at least one ootnote{The $m$-term contribution to the sum in \eqref{Stokes-comp-2nd} is given by  egin{equation}  rac{1}{m!} \prod_{i=1}^{m} \left(\, \sum_{k_{i}=1}^{+\infty} \left(\, \sum_{j=1}^{i-1}k_{j}+1\, ight) S_{-k_{i}}^{F}\, ight) 	au_{1}^{\sum_{j=1}^{m}k_{j}+1}. \end{equation} } $S_{-k'}^F$ with $1<k'<k$. For a general $\ell$ this looks like  egin{equation} \left( -S_{-1}^{Z} ight)^{\ell} = S_{-\ell}^{F} + \cdots +  rac{1}{\ell!}\, \ell!\left( S_{-1}^{F} ight)^{\ell}, \end{equation} 
oindent where the dots indicate terms involving Stokes constants $S_{-k}^F$, $1<k<\ell$, which were already set to zero from previous conditions. By induction, it follows that $S_{-k}^F=0$, for all $k>1$.

For integer values of $\gamma$, the straightforward relation (with $n,m \in \BN^+$)  e  rac{s^n}{\Gamma \left( n+1 ight)} =  rac{md^m}{md s^m}\,  rac{s^{n+m}}{\Gamma \left( n+m+1 ight)} \ee 
oindent directly implies the relation between Borel transforms ootnote{As an historical aside, note that this sort of relations was probably first noticed in \cite{bpv78b}, but, unfortunately, the role of the standard representative---with its (simple) logarithmic ramified structure, and where resurgent analysis becomes most straightforward---was not emphasized enough to spark subsequent further analysis.}  e \CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma']} ] (s) = D_s^{\gamma-\gamma'} \CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} ] (s). \ee 
oindent Assuming that $\gamma-\gamma'$ is an integer and that $\gamma>\gamma'$, the operator $D_s^n$ is herein defined as the $n$th derivative with respect to $s$. The relation \eqref{eq:relation-between-borels-der} also holds if $\gamma<\gamma'$, in which case one simply defines $D_s^n$ as the $n$th 	extit{primitive} ootnote{Each primitive yields an integration constant, and upon multiple primitivation one obtains a polynomial in $s$ with maximum degree $\gamma'-\gamma$. This polynomial is directly related to the first $n_0(\gamma)$ coefficients which were removed from the original asymptotic series $\Phi_{[\gamma]} (\lambda)$ in order to obtain the ``Borel transformable'' asymptotic series $\widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} (\lambda)$.} with respect to $s$. If $\gamma=\gamma'$ then $D_s^0$ is simply the identity. The assumption that $\gamma-\gamma'$ is an integer may be lifted, allowing \eqref{eq:relation-between-borels-der} to be valid for a 	extit{continuous} parameter $\gamma \in \BR$. Let us show how this is done when ootnote{The case where $\gamma<\gamma'$ follows in complete analogy by simply inverting \eqref{eq:relation-between-borels-der} and having the operator $D_s^{\gamma'-\gamma}$, which is now a 	extit{derivative}, acting on $\CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma']} ] (s)$.} $\gamma>\gamma'$. In this case we have to deal with a 	extit{fractional derivative}, where $D_s^\alpha$ is defined, when $0<\alpha<1$, as (see, 	extit{e.g.}, \cite{lot76})  egin{equation} D_s^\alpha f(s) =  rac{1}{\Gamma \left(1-\alphaight)}\,  rac{md}{md s} \int_0^s md t\,  rac{f(t)}{\left( s-t ight)^{\alpha}}. \end{equation} 
oindent This definition naturally extends to any real order $\gamma$ by setting $\gamma = \alpha + m$, with $\gamma \in \BR$, $m \in \BN_+$, and $\alpha$ as above, in which case  e D_s^\gamma f(s) = D_s^\alpha D_s^m f(s) = D_s^\alpha\,  rac{md^m f}{md s^m} (s). \ee 
oindent Applying the fractional derivative to each term in the definition of a Borel transform is straightforward; one only needs to know that, for $f(s) = s^ho$, $ho>-1$, one has  egin{equation} D_s^\alpha s^ho =  rac{\Gamma \left(ho+1ight)}{\Gamma \left(ho+1-\alphaight)}\, s^{ho-\alpha}. \end{equation} 
oindent The action of the fractional derivative $D_s^{\gamma-\gamma'}$ on the Borel transform $\CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} ] (s)$ in \eqref{eq:relation-between-borels-der} can then be easily computed making use of the above expression order-by-order in the series definition of $\CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} ] (s)$. One obtains  e D_s^{\gamma-\gamma'} \CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} ] (s) = \sum_{k=n_0(\gamma)}^{+\infty} \Phi_k\,  rac{s^{\alpha k +  eta + \gamma' - 1}}{\Gamma \left( \alpha k +  eta + \gamma' ight)} \approx \CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma']} ] (s). \ee

We will prove the following claim: there exists $N\ge1$ such that, for any $I \in 
uzh$, the inclusion map $i_N:NI	o I$ induces the zero map $0=(i_N)_*: F_0(NI) 	o F_0(I)$. Once this is done, the statement of the lemma will follow by applying (1) and (3) to the short exact sequence $NI	o I	o I/NI$. As in Corollary~ef{rationalhens}, let $[N]: \mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_night\}^+ 	o \mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_night\}^+$ be the operator that multiplies each $x_i$ by $N$, so that the colimit of the $[N]$, indexed over $N\ge1$ ordered by divisibility, is $\mathbb{Q}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_night\}^+$. By assumption $F_0$ commutes with filtered colimits and annihilates any nonunital henselian $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra; so, given any element $u \in F_0(\mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_night\}^+)$, it follows that there exists $N > 0$ such that $[N]_*(u) = 0$. Now we use that $F_0$ is projectively finitely generated. This means that there exists a surjection \[  igoplus_{i=1}^t \mathbb{Z}[\mathrm{Hom}_{
uzh}(\mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_{n_i}ight\}^+, \cdot)] 	o F_0\] of functors for some $n_1,\dots,n_t\ge1$. For each $i=1,\dots,t$, let $g_i\in F_0(\mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_{n_i}ight\}^+)$ be the image of $\mathrm{id}\in \mathrm{Hom}_{
uzh}(\mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_{n_i}ight\}^+, \mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_{n_i}ight\}^+)$. Then the above surjection concretely means the following: given any $I\in 
uzh$ and any element $y\in F_0(I)$, then $y$ is a finite sum of elements of $F_0(I)$ obtained by pushing forward the $g_i$ along various maps $\phi:\mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_{n_i}ight\}^+ 	o I$. By the second paragraph, there exists an integer $N > 0$ such that $[N]_*(g_i)=0$ for all the generators $g_i$. From this we can easily complete the proof of the claim, as follows. Given any $I \in 
uzh$, every map $\phi: \mathbb{Z}\{ x_1, \dots, x_{n_i}\}^+ 	o N I $ has the property that the composite $i_N \circ \phi: \mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_{n_i}ight\}^+ 	o I$ factors through $[N]$, i.e., one has a commutative diagram in $
uzh$ \[ \xymatrix{ \mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_night\}^+ \ar[r]_-{\phi}\ar[d]^{[N]} & NI \ar[d]^{i_N} \ \mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_night\}^+ \ar[r] & I }.\] By choice of $N$ it follows that the composite \[ \mathbb{Z}[\mathrm{Hom}_{
uzh}( \mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_{n_i}ight\}^+, NI)] 	o F_0(NI) 	o F_0(I) \] is zero. But we have shown that every element of $F_0(NI)$ is a finite sum of elements of the form $\phi_*(g_i)$ for various maps $\phi$. Since $g_i \in F_0( \mathbb{Z}\left\{x_1, \dots, x_{n_i}ight\}^+)$ is annihilated by $[N]$, it follows that $(i_N)_*: F_0(NI) 	o F_0(I)$ is zero, as claimed.

We use the commutative diagram \[ \xymatrix{ V_0 \ar[d] \ar[r] & V_1 \ar[d] \ \Omega^n_R \ar[r]^-{1 - C^{-1}} \ar[d] & \Omega^n_R/ d \Omega^{n-1}_R \ar[d] \ \Omega^n_{R/I} \ar[r]^-{1 - C^{-1}} & \Omega^n_{R/I}/ d \Omega^{n-1}_{R/I} }\] where we define $V_0, V_1$ to be the kernels of the surjective vertical maps $\Omega^n_R 	o \Omega^n_{R/I}$ and $\Omega^n_R/ d \Omega^{n-1}_R 	o \Omega^n_{R/I}/ d \Omega^{n-1}_{R/I}$. We will show that the map $V_0 	o V_1$ is surjective. This easily implies the desired conclusions about the maps $
u^n(R) 	o 
u^{n}(R/I)$ and $\widetilde{
u}^n(R) 	o \widetilde{
u}^n(R/I)$ thanks to the snake lemma. Consider a differential form $\omega = a dx_1 dx_2 \dots dx_n \in \Omega^n_R$ such that one of $\left\{a, x_1, \dots, x_night\}$ belongs to $I$ (here $n = 0$ is allowed). The image of such a class in $\Omega^n_R/d \Omega_R^{n-1}$ belongs to $V_1$, and $V_1$ is generated by such classes. For $u \in R$, we have $$(1 - C^{-1}) ( u \omega) = (u - u^p a^{p-1} x_1^{p-1} \dots x_n^{p-1}) \omega.$$ Since $(R, I)$ is a henselian pair and $a^{p-1} x_1^{p-1} \dots x_n^{p-1}\in I$, we can choose $u \in R$ such that $u - u^p a^{p-1} x_1^{p-1} \dots x_n^{p-1} = 1$, using Lemma~ef{solveeq:hens}. The class $u \omega \in \Omega^n_R$ belongs to $V_0$ and has image given by $\omega$; so $\omega$ is in the image of $V_0 	o V_1$, as desired.

Recall that if $R$ is an $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra, we can make a functorial surjection to $R$ from a polynomial algebra, namely take the polynomial algebra $\mathbb{F}_p[x_a]_{a\in R}$ on variables indexed by the elements of $R$ with the map $x_a\mapsto a$. Let $R'ightarrow R$ denote the henselization of this surjection at its kernel; thus $R'$ is a functorial ind-smooth $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra surjecting onto $R$ with henselian kernel. Moreover, $R\mapsto R'$ evidently commutes with filtered colimits. Define for $m \geq 0$ $$\widetilde{
u^m_r}(R) := H^1(\operatorname{Spec}(R')_\sub{'{e}t};\pi_{m}(\mathcal{TC}/p^r)).$$ Since the coefficient presheaf $\pi_{m}(\TC(-)/p^r)$ commutes with filtered colimits by Corollary ef{TCring}, this 'etale cohomology group commutes with filtered colimits in $R'$ by standard cocontinuity arguments. Combining with the previous, we find that $\widetilde{
u^m_r}(-)$ commutes with filtered colimits. Furthermore, since $\operatorname{Spec}(R')$ has '{e}tale $p$-cohomological dimension $\leq 1$, the descent spectral sequence for $\TC(-)/p^r$ gives a natural map $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)ightarrow \pi_n(\TC(R')/p^r)$ (compare with the proofs of Theorem ef{GHGL} and Theorem ef{padicetaleKthy}). Composing with the map on $\TC$ induced by $R'ightarrow R$ defines the desired natural transformation $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)ightarrow \pi_n(\TC(R)/p^r)$. Now assume $R$ is local. Then $R'$ is too, since a henselian ideal is radical, and it is moreover ind-smooth. Thus the argument recalled before the statement of the theorem, based on the results of Geisser--Levine and Geisser--Hesselholt, shows that $$\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)\oplus \pi_n(K(R')/p^r)\overset{\sim}{ightarrow}\pi_n(\TC(R')/p^r).$$ On the other hand our main rigidity theorem, Theorem ef{mainthm}, gives a long exact sequence $$\ldotsightarrow\pi_n(K(R')/p^r)ightarrow \pi_n(\TC(R')/p^r)\oplus \pi_n(K(R)/p^r)ightarrow \pi_n(\TC(R)/p^r)ightarrow\ldots.$$ Combining shows both that this long exact sequence breaks up into short exact sequences and that $$\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)\oplus\pi_n(K(R)/p^r)\overset{\sim}{ightarrow}\pi_n(\TC(R)/p^r),$$ as claimed.

