We first recall the Courant-Fischer-Weyl Min-Max variational principle concerning the eigenvalues of a compact self-adjoint operator $T$ on a Hilbert space $\mathcal G,$ with positive eigenvalues arranged in the decreasing order $\lambda_0\geq \lambda_1\geq \cdots\geq \lambda_n\geq \cdots ,$ then we have $$\lambda_n = \min_{f\in S_n}\,\,\, \max_{f\in S_n^{\perp},\|f\|_{\mathcal G}=1} < Tf, f>_{\mathcal G},$$ where $S_n$ is a subspace of $\mathcal G$ of dimension $n.$ In our case, we have $T=\mathcal F_c^{\alpha^*}\mathcal F_c^{(\alpha)},$ $\mathcal G= L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha}).$ We consider the special case of $$S_n=\mbox{Span}\left\{\wJ_0, \wJ_1,\ldots,\wJ_{n-1}ight\}$$ and $$f=\ds\sum_{k\geq n} a_k \wJ_k \in S_n^{\perp},\qquad \parallel f\parallel_{L^2_{(I,\omega_\alpha)}}=\ds\sum_{k\geq n}|a_k|^2=1.$$ Here the $ \wJ_{k} $ are the normalized Jacobi polynomials given by \eqref{JacobiP}. From [\cite{NIST} page 456], we have  egin{equation} \| \mathcal F_c^{(\alpha)} \J_k\|_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})} = \parallel \sqrt{\pi} \left( rac{2}{cx}ight) ^{\alpha+1/2}  rac{\Gamma(k+\alpha+1)}{\Gamma(k+1)}J_{k+\alpha+1/2}(cx) \parallel_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})}. \end{equation} and  egin{equation} \parallel x^k \parallel_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})}^2= eta(k+1/2,\alpha+1)\leq \sqrt{ rac{\pi}{e}} rac{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}{k^{\alpha+1}}. \end{equation} By using the well-known bound of the Bessel function given by  egin{equation} |J_{\alpha}(x)|\leq  rac{|x|^{\alpha}}{2^{\alpha}\Gamma(\alpha+1)}\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\; orall\alpha>-1/2, \;\;  orall x \in \mathbb{R}, \end{equation} one gets,  egin{eqnarray} \| \mathcal F_c^{(\alpha)} \wJ_k\|_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})} &\leq &  rac{\sqrt{\pi}\Gamma(k+\alpha+1)}{\sqrt{h_k}\Gamma(k+1)\Gamma(k+\alpha+3/2)}\left( rac{c}{2}ight) ^{k} \parallel x^k \parallel_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})}. 
onumber\ &\leq &  rac{\sqrt{\pi}\Gamma(k+\alpha+1)}{\sqrt{h_k}\Gamma(k+1)\Gamma(k+\alpha+3/2)}\left( rac{c}{2}ight) ^{k}\left( rac{\pi}{e}ight)^{1/4} rac{\sqrt{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}}{k^{ rac{\alpha+1}{2}}} \end{eqnarray} Next, we use \eqref{JacobiP} and the following useful inequalities for the Gamma function, see \cite{Batir}  egin{equation} \sqrt{2e}\left( rac{x+1/2}{e}ight)^{x+1/2}\leq\Gamma(x+1)\leq \sqrt{2\pi}\left( rac{x+1/2}{e}ight)^{x+1/2}\;,\;\;\; x>0. \end{equation} Then, we have  egin{eqnarray} \| \mathcal F_c^{(\alpha)} \wJ_k\|_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})} &\leq &  rac{\sqrt{\pi}\sqrt{(k+\alpha+ rac{1}{2})\Gamma(k+2\alpha+1)}}{2^{\alpha+ rac{1}{2}}\sqrt{\Gamma(k+1)}\Gamma(k+\alpha+ rac{3}{2})}\left( rac{c}{2}ight) ^{k}\left( rac{\pi}{e}ight)^{ rac{1}{4}} rac{\sqrt{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}}{k^{ rac{\alpha+1}{2}}}
onumber\ &=& rac{\pi \sqrt{\Gamma(\alpha+1)} ig(k+2\alpha+ rac{1}{2}\Big)^{ rac{k}{2}+\alpha+ rac{1}{4 }}}{\sqrt{ec}2^{\alpha}k^{ rac{\alpha+1}{2}}\left(k+ rac{1}{2}ight)^{ rac{k}{2}+ rac{1}{4}}\left(k+\alpha+1ight)^{k+\alpha+ rac{1}{2}}} \left( rac{ce}{2}ight) ^{k+ rac{1}{2 }}
onumber\ &=& rac{\pi \sqrt{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}}{\sqrt{ec}2^{\alpha}k^{ rac{\alpha+1}{2}}}\left(1+ rac{\alpha- rac{1}{2}}{k+\alpha+1}ight)^{ rac{k}{2}+\alpha+ rac{1}{4}} \left( rac{ce}{2k+1}ight) ^{k+ rac{1}{2}}. \end{eqnarray} Since the function $g_a(x)=\left(1+ rac{a}{x}ight)^{a+x}$ for $x\geq1$ and $a>-1$ is decreasing on $[1,+\infty[$. Then, we have  egin{eqnarray} \| \mathcal F_c^{(\alpha)} \wJ_k\|_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})} &\leq & rac{\pi \sqrt{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}}{\sqrt{c}k^{ rac{\alpha+1}{2}}} \left( rac{ce}{2k+1}ight) ^{k+ rac{1}{2}}. \end{eqnarray} Hence, For the previous $f\in S_n^{\perp},$ and by using H"older's inequality, combined with the Minkowski's inequality for an infinite sums, and taking into account that $\|f\|_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})}= 1,$ so that $|a_k|\leq 1,$ for $k\geq n,$ one gets:  egin{eqnarray} |< \mathcal F_c^{\alpha^*}\mathcal F_c^{(\alpha)} f,f>_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})}|&=&|< \mathcal F_c^{(\alpha)} f,\mathcal F_c^{(\alpha)}f>_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})}| \leq \sum_{k\geq n} |a_k|^2 \| \mathcal F_c^{(\alpha)} \wJ_k\|^2_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})}\ &\leq&\left( rac{2^ rac{1}{4}\pi \sqrt{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}}{\sqrt{ec}} \sum_{k\geq n} \left( rac{ec}{2k+1}ight)^{k+1/2} rac{1}{k^{ rac{\alpha+1}{2}}}ight)^2 \end{eqnarray} The decay of the sequence appearing in the previous sum, allows us to compare this later with its integral counterpart, that is  egin{equation} \sum_{k\geq n} \left( rac{ec}{2k+1}ight)^{k+1/2} rac{1}{k^{ rac{\alpha+1}{2}}} \leq \int_{n-1}^{\infty} rac{ e^{-(x+1/2) \log( rac{2x+1}{ec})}}{x^{ rac{\alpha+1}{2}}}\, dx\leq \int_{n-1}^{\infty} rac{ e^{-(x+1/2)\log( rac{2n-1}{ec})}}{(n-1)^{ rac{\alpha+1}{2}}}\, dx. \end{equation} Hence, by using (ef{Eq3.3}) and (ef{Eq3.4}), one concludes that  egin{eqnarray} \max_{f\in S_n^{\perp},\, \|f\|_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})=1}} < \mathcal F_c^{(\alpha)} f, \mathcal F_c^{(\alpha)} f>^{1/2}_{L^2(I,\omega_{\alpha})}&\leq & rac{2^ rac{1}{4}\pi \sqrt{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}}{\sqrt{ec}}  rac{1}{\log( rac{